{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-253547",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The infimum of a set $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nIf $B$ is a finite set, the infimum of $B$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=0,1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}+1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{(1)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(2)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(3)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(4)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nB=\\{1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{10},\\,\\frac{1}{17}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(B) & =min\\{1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{10},\\,\\frac{1}{17}\\}=\\frac{1}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
